# ProtoMap Server configuration.
#
# Run ./self-hosted/scripts/install.sh from the root of the clone to generate .env in this folder, or copy this file to
# .env and fill in the values yourself. Keep .env private: it contains secrets.

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# At least 16 characters that create the owner's account in the platform, while the server has no users. Generate
# one with: openssl rand -hex 32
PROTOMAP_SETUP_KEY=

# 32 random bytes in hexadecimal that encrypt the secrets the server stores, such as AI provider API keys. Generate one
# with: openssl rand -hex 32. Back it up with the database: without it, stored API keys cannot be read.
PROTOMAP_SECRET_KEY=

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database (pick one of the two options)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Option 1: the bundled PostgreSQL, which Docker Compose runs (recommended for trying ProtoMap). Change the user,
# password, and database before the first start of any deployment other than a local test; an existing volume keeps
# the credentials it was created with. Use only ASCII letters, numbers, hyphens, periods, underscores, and tildes.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=postgres
POSTGRES_USER=protomap
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=protomap
POSTGRES_DB=protomap
PROTOMAP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://${POSTGRES_USER}:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@postgres:5432/${POSTGRES_DB}

# Option 2: your own PostgreSQL 18 or newer (recommended for production). Remove the bundled settings above and set
# your provider's connection URL. The first start needs permission to create tables.
#PROTOMAP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/database

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A personal API key that setup gives the owner it creates, for the CLI on the computer that runs a trial installation:
# its ID, a UUID, and the SHA-256 of the key in hexadecimal. install.sh sets both and writes the key itself to the CLI's
# login, ~/.protomap/login.json. Set both or neither.
#PROTOMAP_INITIAL_API_KEY_ID=
#PROTOMAP_INITIAL_API_KEY_HASH=

# The release of the hounddogai/protomap image to run, such as 1.2.3. The latest release by default.
#PROTOMAP_VERSION=latest

# The published port.
#PROTOMAP_PORT=3300

# Leave unset to publish the port on every interface. Set 127.0.0.1 to accept connections only from the Docker host,
# the computer that runs this stack, such as from a proxy on it that ends TLS.
#PROTOMAP_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# The addresses or CIDR ranges, separated by spaces, of proxies in front of the stack whose X-Forwarded-Proto and
# X-Forwarded-Host headers to believe, beyond private networks and the Docker host, which are always trusted. Set it
# when a proxy that ends TLS reaches the stack from a public address, such as a cloud load balancer.
#PROTOMAP_TRUSTED_PROXIES=203.0.113.0/24

# A password of at least eight characters for the job queue dashboard at http://127.0.0.1:8801, where the user is
# "admin". Only the Docker host reaches it; from another computer, forward the port over SSH first:
# ssh -L 8801:127.0.0.1:8801 <Docker host>. Leave unset to turn the dashboard off. A shorter password turns it off too,
# with an error in the worker's log.
#PROTOMAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# The port the job queue dashboard is published on, on 127.0.0.1 of the Docker host.
#PROTOMAP_DASHBOARD_PORT=8801

# The port the bundled PostgreSQL is published on, on 127.0.0.1 of the Docker host. Docker picks a free port by default;
# find it with: docker compose --file self-hosted/compose.yaml port postgres 5432
#PROTOMAP_POSTGRES_PORT=5432

# How many megabytes of memory the artifacts the server and the worker each keep decoded may take, so queries do not
# decode the whole graph each time. 768 by default.
#PROTOMAP_ARTIFACT_CACHE_MB=768

# How many connections the database pools of the server and the worker each keep open at most. 10 by default. Each chat
# response the server runs holds one more of its own, so PostgreSQL must accept twice this, plus
# PROTOMAP_MAX_RESPONSES, plus a few.
#PROTOMAP_DATABASE_CONNECTIONS=10

# How many chat responses the server runs at once. 32 by default. A message sent past it is answered that the server is
# busy, to try again in a moment.
#PROTOMAP_MAX_RESPONSES=32

# The address people open ProtoMap at, which links in email start with. Setup records the address it ran at, and server
# owners change it in Settings › Server › Address; set this to fix it here instead.
#PROTOMAP_PUBLIC_URL=https://protomap.example.com

# The SMTP server that sends invitations and password resets, with whom the email is from. Server owners can set it in
# Settings › Server › Email instead; set it here to fix it. Use smtps:// for TLS (port 465), smtp:// for STARTTLS (587),
# or smtp://host:25?tls=none for a server on a trusted network. Escape @ and : in the user and password as %40 and %3A.
#PROTOMAP_SMTP_URL=smtp://user:password@smtp.example.com:587
#PROTOMAP_SMTP_FROM=ProtoMap <protomap@example.com>

# The header that tells the server each client's address, which limits count by: failed logins besides the email, codes
# an identity provider refuses, wrong CLI login codes besides the user, CLI logins under way, and forgotten passwords'
# resets. Counting one address that stands for everyone would make everyone wait for one person's failures, so set this
# only where clients' own addresses arrive:
# - X-Forwarded-For counts the address that reached the stack, which suits a stack that people reach directly, on a
#   Docker host that keeps clients' addresses, as rootful Docker Engine on Linux does for IPv4. Docker Desktop, rootless
#   Docker, and clients over IPv6 when the stack's Docker network has none reach the stack from one address of Docker's
#   own: leave this unset there.
# - Behind a proxy that ends TLS, set PROTOMAP_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1 and name the header that proxy sets to the
#   client's address, such as X-Real-IP, or leave this unset. X-Forwarded-For would count the proxy's own address.
#PROTOMAP_CLIENT_ADDRESS_HEADER=X-Forwarded-For

# Where the worker fetches the catalog of AI models ProtoMap offers, hourly, so the models in the model picker stay
# current without an upgrade. Point it at a copy you control, such as on your network when the server cannot reach the
# internet, or set "off" to keep the catalog this release bundles. https://protomap.ai/model-catalog-v1.json by default.
#PROTOMAP_MODEL_CATALOG_URL=https://protomap.ai/model-catalog-v1.json

# Set to "on" to serve the API reference at /api/docs, with the API's OpenAPI document at /api/openapi.json, to anyone
# who reaches the server. Off by default.
#PROTOMAP_API_DOCS=on
